# Line-ending policy — normalise text to LF in the repository AND in the working tree.
#
# Why this file exists (#327):
#
# The repo ran `core.autocrlf=true` with no `.gitattributes`, and a handful of files
# (`CHANGELOG.md`, `specs/roadmap.md`) had been committed with CRLF *stored in the blob*.
# That combination means git renormalises the whole file on any commit that touches it, so
# a nine-line CHANGELOG entry lands as a ~2500-line diff. PR #326 had to isolate the
# normalisation into its own commit just to keep its real change reviewable — a workaround
# that would otherwise be needed on every future docs change.
#
# `eol=lf` (not just `text=auto`) also pins the WORKING TREE to LF, which removes a second,
# long-standing papercut documented in CLAUDE.md: Prettier defaults to `endOfLine: "lf"`, so
# on a CRLF checkout `npm run format:check` fails against dozens of files nobody touched.
# That artifact has cost real debugging time and trained people to ignore a real gate. With
# this file, local `format:check` agrees with CI on Linux.
#
# No tracked `.bat`, `.cmd` or `.ps1` files exist, so pinning LF everywhere is safe here.
# Revisit this line if a Windows-only script is ever added — those need CRLF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets — never touch. The five PNGs below are what git currently detects as binary;
# the rest are listed pre-emptively so adding one later cannot silently corrupt it.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
